{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>מְצַחֵק (mocking)</b> — the language of idolatry, the language of murder, the language of sexual immorality. He was quarreling with Isaac over the inheritance, saying, 'I am the firstborn and I take a double portion,' and they would go out to the field and he would take his bow and shoot arrows at him, as it is said: 'As a madman who casts firebrands, arrows, and death, so is the man who deceives his neighbor and says, Am I not in jest?' (Proverbs 26:18–19). And from Sarah's response you learn: 'For the son of this maidservant shall not inherit,' etc. — all this is the language of Rashi. And here too the Rav writes all the disputations, for it is taught (Tosefta Sotah 6:3): Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar said (in our version: ben Yochai), four things did Rabbi Akiva interpret, and I do not interpret as he did, and my words appear more reasonable than his. Rabbi Akiva interpreted: 'And Sarah saw the son of Hagar the Egyptian, whom she had borne to Abraham, מְצַחֵק' — צְחוֹק means nothing other than the language of idolatry, etc. But I say: heaven forbid that such a thing should be in the house of a righteous man! Is it possible that someone regarding whom it is written, 'For I have known him, to the end that he may command his children and his household,' etc. (Genesis 18:19) — that there should be in his house idolatry and sexual immorality and bloodshed?! Rather, the צְחוֹק mentioned here is only the language of inheritance, for from the hour that Isaac was born everyone was rejoicing. Ishmael said to them: Fools! I am the firstborn and I take a double portion — as from the response that Sarah our mother said to Abraham you learn, etc. And my words appear more reasonable than his. And likewise the expression that the Rav said, 'quarreling with Isaac over the inheritance,' does not appear correct, for if so, this would have to be after many days, and Ishmael would have been very old when his mother carried him on her shoulder, and so the Sages said (Yalkut Shimoni §95) that he was seventeen years old — if so, it was in the year that Isaac was weaned. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 21:9) according to the plain sense that מְצַחֵק means amusing himself as is the way of every youth, and she was jealous of him because he was older than her son. And what seems correct in my eyes is that this was on the day of Isaac's weaning, and she saw him mocking Isaac or the great feast. And therefore Scripture says 'the son of Hagar the Egyptian' and does not say 'Ishmael' מְצַחֵק, and likewise she said <b>Cast out this maidservant and her son</b>, for she said: the servant who mocks his master deserves to die or to be flogged, but I want only that you expel him from me, and that he not inherit your property at all together with my son, who is the son of a noblewoman. And she said that he should expel his mother as well, for the youth cannot leave his mother — 'and he left his mother and died.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The final phrase 'ועזב את אמו ומת' is rendered hyper-literally; it is textually obscure — it seems to allude to a midrashic tradition about Ishmael's separation from his mother and eventual death, but the syntax is elliptic and possibly corrupt."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:33:03.307165098Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}